A big part of this discussion is triggered by the term "abuse" which
is not a well defined term with a generally accepted meaning by the
nethack community. The DevTeam (may they live long and prosper) are
the ultimate arbitraters of what is, and what isn't, an abuse. Thus, 
they decided that polypiling for unlimited wands of wishing was an
abuse and fixed that. After some amount of time (a dozend years 
according to some of the posts in this thread) they have not yet
decided that pudding farming and/or the death drop is an abuse, and
so, have not fixed it.

The fact that some players don't like pudding farming and label it
an abuse, doesn't actually make it one. And what about the
consequences of this so-called abuse? I see three different scenerios
here: single user systems, mutliuser systems and public servers, and
nethack tournaments, such as the recent devnull tournament.

On a single user system, farm or don't farm. Who cares? It's your
system and your high score list. No body else gets hurt.

For a tournament, the tournament organizers are free to adopt
whatever awards they choose and can incorporate the current 
nethack score in whatever fashion they choose. This year's devnull
scoring changes made a nice step in this direction, but probably
can, and will be, improverd even more next year. I think the awards
for first, fastest, lowest scored, and best-behaved ascensions 
should be extended to the individual role awards as well. For example,
instead of 5 points for highest score for foo, I think it should be
more like 1 point for highest scored foo ascension, 2 points for 
first foo ascension, 3 points for fastest foo ascension, 4 points 
for lowest scored foo ascension, and the best behaved foo ascension
should get 1 point for each kept conduct. (I guess these points
only count in determining Best In Show Clan). I think that would
do a much better job at recognizing actual nethack skill levels. 

The situation on a multiuser system or a public server is a little
harder to deal with. I think (and this is just my opionion, YMMV)
that the basis of the problem is that some (not all, by any means)
of the skilled players players resent the fact that unskilled
pudding farmers (like me) can achieve high ranks in the high score
list while their more elegant accomplishments go more or less
unhearled. Unless or until the DevTeam (may they live long and
prosper) decide to change the game scoring mechanism, you're just
going to have to live with it. So just suck it up and take delight
in your accomplishments and let us score whores have our fun. I
mean, after all, it's only a game.

